{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fourier heat conduction law fourier heat conduction law","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","fileName":"Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fourier Heat Conduction Law","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 00:55\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Conduction Convection and Radiation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","Energy Conservation in Thermal Continua","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"fourier heat conduction law fourier heat conduction law","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","fileName":"Fourier Heat Conduction Law.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Fourier Heat Conduction Law","heading":"Fourier Heat Conduction Law","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Fourier's law relates conductive heat rate to thermal conductivity, area normal to the flow, and the temperature gradient. In one dimension, the negative sign in $q=-kA\\,dT/dx$ records that heat flows toward decreasing temperature.\n\nThermal conductivity is the material proportionality factor, while geometry and the temperature field supply the remaining terms. The law becomes part of the [[Energy Conservation in Thermal Continua|energy equation]] when conductive inflow and outflow are balanced against generation and storage.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Conduction Convection and Radiation","Energy Conservation in Thermal Continua","cosmolheattransfermodels.pdf"],"pr